### What is the Hexagonal architecture?

Hexagonal (Ports and Adapters) places domain logic at the center, defines ports (interfaces) for everything it needs or offers, and attaches adapters for specific technologies. The dependency rule is that everything points inward.

### How do ports and adapters work?

```java
// OUTBOUND PORT: domain declares what it needs
package com.bank.domain.port;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface LoanRepository {
    void save(Loan loan);
    Loan findById(String loanId);
}

public interface CreditBureauPort {
    int fetchScore(String customerId);
}
```

```java
// INBOUND PORT: domain declares what it offers
package com.bank.domain.port;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface ApproveLoanUseCase {
    LoanDecision approve(String customerId, BigDecimal amount);
}
```

```java
// DOMAIN SERVICE: depends only on ports (interfaces)
package com.bank.domain.service;

import com.bank.domain.port.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LoanApprovalService implements ApproveLoanUseCase {

    private final CreditBureauPort creditBureau;
    private final LoanRepository loanRepo;

    public LoanApprovalService(CreditBureauPort creditBureau, LoanRepository loanRepo) {
        this.creditBureau = creditBureau;
        this.loanRepo = loanRepo;
    }

    @Override
    public LoanDecision approve(String customerId, BigDecimal amount) {
        int score = creditBureau.fetchScore(customerId);
        if (score < 700) return LoanDecision.rejected("Score below 700");
        Loan loan = new Loan(customerId, amount);
        loanRepo.save(loan);
        return LoanDecision.approved(loan);
    }
}
```

```java
// OUTBOUND ADAPTER: implements the port with real technology
package com.bank.adapter.out;

import com.bank.domain.port.LoanRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;

public interface JpaLoanRepository extends JpaRepository<Loan, String>, LoanRepository {
    // Spring Data implements save() and findById() automatically
}
```

```java
// INBOUND ADAPTER: REST controller drives the domain through the inbound port
package com.bank.adapter.in;

import com.bank.domain.port.ApproveLoanUseCase;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/loans")
public class LoanController {

    private final ApproveLoanUseCase approveUseCase;

    public LoanController(ApproveLoanUseCase approveUseCase) {
        this.approveUseCase = approveUseCase;
    }

    @PostMapping
    public LoanDecision apply(@RequestParam String customerId,
                              @RequestParam BigDecimal amount) {
        return approveUseCase.approve(customerId, amount);
    }
}
```

### How do you test the domain in isolation?

```java
// Unit test with in-memory fakes: zero database, zero Spring
class LoanApprovalServiceTest {
    @Test
    void rejectsLowScore() {
        CreditBureauPort fakeBureau = id -> 600; // always returns 600
        LoanRepository fakeRepo = new InMemoryLoanRepository();
        var service = new LoanApprovalService(fakeBureau, fakeRepo);

        LoanDecision result = service.approve("CUST-1001", new BigDecimal("5000000"));

        assertEquals("REJECTED", result.getStatus());
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Test passed: rejectsLowScore
```

### Why is hexagonal a style layered ON TOP of microservices, not an alternative?

Microservices and monolith are deployment choices (how many units, how they communicate). Hexagonal is about code organization inside one unit. You do not choose between them; you apply hexagonal inside each service.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: domain imports Spring JPA
package com.bank.domain.service;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository; // BREAKS the architecture

// CORRECT: domain imports only its own port interface
package com.bank.domain.service;
import com.bank.domain.port.LoanRepository; // domain owns this interface
```

### When should you NOT use hexagonal?

For trivial CRUD services where the domain logic is just pass-through, hexagonal adds ceremony without benefit. Apply it where the domain is rich and valuable -- loan approval, settlement, fraud scoring.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The ports belong to the domain package, which enforces the inward dependency direction at compile time.
- If your domain imports JPA, Spring, or HTTP classes, you have broken the architecture.
- You can enforce this with ArchUnit tests in CI.
- The benefits are testability (test domain with fakes), technology independence (swap DB by writing a new adapter), and clarity (business rules untangled from framework annotations).

### How do you enforce the dependency rule with ArchUnit?

ArchUnit tests in CI prevent accidental imports of framework classes into the domain package. If a developer adds a Spring annotation to a domain entity, the build fails.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

import com.tngtech.archunit.core.importer.ClassFileImporter;
import com.tngtech.archunit.lang.ArchRule;
import static com.tngtech.archunit.lang.syntax.ArchRuleDefinition.noClasses;

public class HexagonalArchTest {

    // @Test
    public void domainMustNotDependOnSpringOrJpa() {
        var classes = new ClassFileImporter()
            .importPackages("com.bank");

        ArchRule rule = noClasses()
            .that().resideInAPackage("..domain..")
            .should().dependOnClassesThat()
            .resideInAnyPackage(
                "org.springframework..",
                "javax.persistence..",
                "jakarta.persistence..");

        rule.check(classes);
    }
}
```

```text
// Build output when a developer adds @Entity to a domain class:
// ArchUnit test FAILED:
//   com.bank.domain.model.Loan imports jakarta.persistence.Entity
//   Domain must not depend on JPA!
//   Fix: move @Entity to the adapter or use a separate JPA entity in adapter.out
```
